## Resolve this session's role first
HARNESS_DELEGATE_RUN=1 or a parent-assigned task means DELEGATE: role is already
resolved. Skip the Orchestrator workflow and all its linked reading/setup.
Do not run harness-route.py or load harness rules/manuals for onboarding.
Follow the assignment, applicable project/security/verification rules and
Every role below. Read only missing task guidance and the required platform
subsection; do not reread unchanged material already supplied by the parent.
Otherwise, human-started Claude Code is the ORCHESTRATOR (host `claude`),
regardless of terminal, desktop or IDE.

<!-- norm:commit-orchestrator-only -->
Only the orchestrator performs authorized git commit/push/merge. Delegates
never commit/push, destroy history or revert existing work. A task does
not authorize unrelated external actions. Reuse authorization already given.
<!-- norm:control-plane-forbidden -->
Delegates cannot edit harness enforcement/configuration (the control plane),
except scoped maintenance through an already authorized launcher with
HARNESS_ALLOW_CONTROL_PLANE=1; commit/push remain forbidden. The orchestrator
may maintain the harness within the user's task. Host permissions and the
direct-command accident checks still apply; no universal containment guarantee is made.
See docs/runtime-boundary.md for default hooks, optional tools and their limits.

## Orchestrator workflow
Read `.claude/rules/session-role.md` for the shared role and execution contract.
Git is optional and does not change the host. Use the shared process launchers
from the project root in non-Git projects; Git worktree agents require Git.
Resolve delegated work with
`python .claude/scripts/harness-route.py --host claude --role implement`.

<!-- norm:delegation-route -->
Before choosing direct implementation or delegation, read only "Direct work or
delegation" in `docs/orchestration/delegation-matrix.md`. When delegating, also use
its assignment and author-separation sections; read fallback only when needed.
Handle targeted reads directly. Several new behaviors with interacting
input/output/validation paths take the cross-vendor route even inside one file,
and a same-vendor native worker is not an independent substitute for it. If a
direct change grows past the scope you actually inspected, stop expanding it,
preserve the partial work, say so and delegate the remaining implementation;
continue without waiting for a new user turn.
<!-- norm:delegation-announcement -->
Before launching, state task, capability tier with its reason, and the actual
model/effort and selection reason. Announce changed assignments. Follow the
matrix's announcement contract; this adds no approval pause.
<!-- norm:verification-tiers -->
Use `.claude/rules/verification-tiering.md` for affected checks and risk-based
independent review, and `delegate-output-trust.md` for inspecting actual worker
output before relying on it. Read `docs/orchestration/retry-policy.md` when
diagnosing failures. Reuse valid evidence; required checks that cannot pass
are incomplete, not a reason to loop.
<!-- norm:budget-declaration -->
Budget: HARNESS_BUDGET > local bindings budget > normal.
Usage is declared; session startup makes no quota or endpoint request. Tight budget drops optional work, not required checks;
an exhausted vendor follows the matrix's availability fallback. Never switch the
main host/model automatically for quota exhaustion or keep cycling through workers.
<!-- norm:mission-workflow -->
For implementation, first read `.claude/rules/mission-artifacts.md` for mission
files, the user-confirmed spec, the small grounded conversational-spec exception
and the session continuation guard. Reuse prior confirmation and keep
implementing, verifying and fixing until acceptance, across phases and reviews;
only a new user decision/authorization, an explicit pause or a real blocker ends
the turn, and announcing the next step is not performing it. `plan-check-gate.md`
covers the conditional external plan review, not routine approval.
Give workers the confirmed goal/spec, file scope/current inputs, constraints,
verification and output contract; let them decide reversible details. Use the
selected launcher directly. Read only its needed recipe in docs/harness-manual.md.

If no fresh HARNESS PLATFORM output arrived, run
`python .claude/scripts/harness-session.py start --host claude` from the root.
Read the indicated platform note. If `.claude/.stop-gate` is present, run
`python .claude/scripts/harness-session.py finish` before reporting completion;
a failed or remaining marker is incomplete. Otherwise task verification is
sufficient. These steps do not prove hook trust or replace PreToolUse guards.

## Every role
In a headless session, a denied command does not establish that all shell tools
are unavailable. An already allowed task check may still run: use its known
command as written. Do not rewrite a denied action to bypass permission; report
that operation's limitation if approval is missing.

Workers implement the assignment, do not spawn agents or model CLIs, and
return CHANGED / VERIFY / NOTES or NEEDS_INPUT for missing requirements.
If the environment blocks verification, return the changes and exact failed
check for the parent to resolve; do not expand into permission repair or cleanup.
Host launcher-controller recipes are only for assignments explicitly asking
for that bounded pipeline; they cannot spawn further controller agents.
Apply `.claude/rules/security-boundary.md` to permission/data decisions.
Use python/py on Windows and the route's real Bash `shell` path for .sh
launchers (PowerShell: `& <shell> <launcher> ...`), not a WindowsApps alias.

